    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d114">

                <pb facs="0228" n="128" xml:id="pg128"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.72/11: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-22T08:00:00-00:44"><hi rendition="#italic">December 22,
                                1920—8 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 4:54 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>98. Department’s 61, November 26th, 6 p.m.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d114-f1"/>
                    Financial Adviser in letter dated December 3rd, to the Secretary of State
                    recommended that Liberian Government should not give favorable consideration to
                    the applications for concessions now before Legislature. President King in his
                    annual message delivered to Legislature December 15th, recommended that no
                    concessions be granted pending the negotiations with the Government of the
                    United States relative to five million dollar loan credit.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
